---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import OneRepMaxCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/OneRepMaxCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="one-rep-max-calculator">
  <OneRepMaxCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the 1RM calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Switch to kilograms or pounds. The page remembers your choice for next time.</li>
      <li>Enter the weight you lifted, including the bar.</li>
      <li>
        Enter how many clean reps you completed. Two to five reps taken close to failure gives the most
        accurate estimate.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the rounding increment to match your smallest pair of plates, so you can load each number
        in the percentage table.
      </li>
      <li>Read your estimated max, then work from the percentage table for programming.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The four formulas</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Formula</th>
            <th scope="col">Equation</th>
            <th scope="col">Behaviour</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Epley (1985)</td>
            <td>1RM = w × (1 + r ÷ 30)</td>
            <td>The most widely used. Linear, and reads high above about 8 reps</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Brzycki (1993)</td>
            <td>1RM = w × 36 ÷ (37 − r)</td>
            <td>Conservative at higher reps. The basis of most printed percentage charts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Lombardi (1989)</td>
            <td>1RM = w × r^0.10</td>
            <td>A power curve, not a line. The flattest of the four</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>O'Conner (1989)</td>
            <td>1RM = w × (1 + 0.025 × r)</td>
            <td>A simple 2.5% per rep rule. Almost identical to Epley at low reps</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      At three reps the four models are usually within 2 percent of each other. At ten reps the spread
      can reach 8 percent. So this one rep max calculator shows all four and averages them.
    </p>
    <p>
      At a single rep, Epley and O'Conner do not return the input weight exactly (Epley gives 103.3
      percent of it). A single is a one rep max, so for one rep the calculator returns your entered
      weight for all four models.
    </p>

    <h2>Reps to percentage of 1RM</h2>
    <p>
      Most programmes are built on this table. The reps column is Brzycki's equation rearranged:
      reps ≈ 37 − 36 × (fraction of 1RM).
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">% of 1RM</th>
            <th scope="col">Approx. reps</th>
            <th scope="col">What it is used for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>100%</td><td>1</td><td>Testing only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>95%</td><td>2</td><td>Heavy singles and doubles, peaking work</td></tr>
          <tr><td>90%</td><td>4</td><td>Strength triples</td></tr>
          <tr><td>85%</td><td>6</td><td>The classic 5×5 zone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>80%</td><td>8</td><td>Strength and hypertrophy overlap</td></tr>
          <tr><td>75%</td><td>10</td><td>Hypertrophy, sets of 8–10</td></tr>
          <tr><td>70%</td><td>12</td><td>Volume work</td></tr>
          <tr><td>65%</td><td>14</td><td>Technique and back-off sets</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60%</td><td>16</td><td>Speed work and warm-ups</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50%</td><td>19</td><td>Warm-up and rehab loading</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Use the rep column as an average, not a rule. Rep capacity at a given percentage varies a lot
      between people and between lifts. Leg press and squat allow many more reps at 80 percent than a
      bench press, and beginners usually do fewer reps at a given percentage than experienced lifters.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting an accurate estimate</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Use a set taken close to failure. A set of 5 with 3 reps left gives a much too low estimate.
        Aim for 0 to 1 reps in reserve.
      </li>
      <li>
        Stay in the 2–5 rep range where you can. There, all four models agree and the physiology is
        closest to a true max.
      </li>
      <li>
        Count only clean reps. Grinding, bouncing or changed technique increases the rep count and so
        the estimate.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep conditions consistent: same equipment, similar time of day, similar warm-up, similar
        fatigue. A fresh Monday set and a Friday set after squats are not comparable.
      </li>
      <li>
        Estimate per lift. You cannot calculate your bench 1RM from your deadlift, and the formulas
        behave differently across lifts.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the estimate behaves across lifts</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Lift</th>
            <th scope="col">How well rep-based estimates work</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Back squat</td><td>Good. Large muscle mass, high rep capacity at a given percentage</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Deadlift</td><td>Good at low reps. Grip and lower-back fatigue make high-rep sets predict too low</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bench press</td><td>Usually predicts slightly too high. Most people do fewer reps at 85% than the chart says</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Overhead press</td><td>Over-predicts. Small muscle mass and a short range of usable percentages</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Olympic lifts</td><td>Unreliable. Snatch and clean and jerk are technique-limited, and multi-rep sets are rarely maximal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Machines and isolation</td><td>Rough at best. Rep capacity is very high and varies with the machine's strength curve</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Should you ever test a true max?</h2>
    <p>
      Competitive lifters do, because competition requires it. Most other people should not. A true
      single needs a long warm-up and a spotter or safety pins, costs days of recovery, and has the
      highest injury risk in the gym. A hard set of three through these formulas gives a number
      accurate enough to programme from, and you get it from a normal session.
    </p>
    <p>
      Estimate again every four to six weeks. Strength does not change fast enough to check more often,
      and constant testing takes time from the training that makes progress.
    </p>

    <h2>Using the number safely</h2>
    <p>
      An estimated max is a planning tool. On a given day your actual capacity changes by 5 to 10
      percent with sleep, food, stress and your position in a training block. Autoregulate: if the
      planned weight moves badly, take some off. Warm up fully, use collars, set the safety pins, and
      stop the set when form breaks, not when the rep count says so.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
